    kind: work_item  # b979
    title: "A clock read is host_info_read in EVERY language, not just rust: fifteen catalogues need clock rows or the vocabulary means different things per language"  # b979
    status: todo_hard  # b979
    priority: 2  # b979
    parent:  # b979
    tags:  # b979
    - io_boundary  # b979
    - verify_claims  # b979
    - catalogue  # b979
    - vocabulary  # b979
    isbefore: []  # b979
    duplicate_of: []  # b979
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b979
    pr_ref:  # b979
    description: "FOLLOWS FROM THE WI-pavob RULING 2026-08-26: \"yes in\" -- a clock read is\nhost_info_read. That is a statement about the VOCABULARY, not about Rust, so it\nbinds every language the tool catalogues.\n\nWHY THIS IS FILED SEPARATELY rather than folded into WI-lutuh's rust half: the\nrust half is an ENUMERATION of one language's stdlib. This is a vocabulary\nchange that lands rows in fifteen catalogues. Folding it in would hide a\ncross-language change inside a per-language audit, and would let host_info_read\nmean different things per language for however long the other fourteen lag --\nwhich is precisely the drift the axis discipline exists to prevent.\n\nWHAT IS OWED. host_info_read rows for the clock surface of every catalogued\nlanguage. Non-exhaustive, from memory rather than measured, so the first task is\nto MEASURE the real surface per language rather than trust this list:\n  rust        std::time::SystemTime::now, std::time::Instant::now (already\n              scoped on WI-pavob; measured against the installed toolchain)\n  python      time.time, time.monotonic, time.perf_counter, time.time_ns,\n              datetime.datetime.now / utcnow\n  go          time.Now, time.Since\n  java        System.currentTimeMillis, System.nanoTime, Instant.now\n  javascript  Date.now, new Date(), performance.now\n  and the remaining catalogued languages.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE BAR IS THE SAME as the stdlib audit's: total surface (every clock\nthe module exposes, not the ones a sample repo calls), EXACT matching, and\nrefuse-by-default. A monotonic clock counts: on Linux CLOCK_MONOTONIC runs from\nboot, so an early read leaks approximate uptime, which distinguishes machines.\n\nTWO THINGS THE IMPLEMENTER SHOULD EXPECT:\n  1. host_info_read WILL FIRE ALMOST UNIVERSALLY after this. Nearly every\n     program reads a clock, so \"never reads host information\" becomes false for\n     anything that timestamps a log line. That is the honest answer and was\n     accepted on the record when the ruling was made -- but it means this\n     category's discriminating power drops sharply, and a bakeoff will show it.\n     Do not read that as a regression.\n  2. IT IS A RECALL-INCREASING CHANGE, so it points the OPPOSITE way from the\n     usual false-inconclusive work: it makes MORE claims fail, not fewer. It\n     therefore does NOT need the cross-language measurement that the standing\n     rule demands of gate-LOOSENING changes -- but its effect on existing\n     verdicts should still be measured before it lands, because it will move\n     them."  # b979
    fields: {}  # b979
- op: discuss  # 4132
  at: '2026-08-26T22:34:22Z'  # 4132
  by: agent  # 4132
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4132
  clock: 2  # 4132
  nonce: '4132'  # 4132
  message: "SEQUENCING AGAINST THE OWNER'S ARC, decided 2026-08-26 at the owner's request.\nThe arc is:\n\n    INV-pusin -> WI-lutuh + INV-zimud -> INV-vivok -> WI-fosir / INV-hosul -> P7\n\nTHIS ITEM IS NOT A NEW ARC STAGE. It is a required COMPONENT of stage 2.\n`module_completeness: complete` asserts that EVERY I/O primitive a module\nexposes is catalogued. A clock is now I/O (WI-pavob, \"yes in\"), so any module\nexposing a clock cannot be declared complete until its clock rows exist. For\nrust that work already sits inside WI-pavob and is ruled. For the other fourteen\nlanguages it gets absorbed into each language's WI-lutuh decomposition as that\ndecomposition happens. This item exists so no language is silently skipped, NOT\nso it can be scheduled on its own.\n\nTHE REAL CONSTRAINT IS A MEASUREMENT CONFOUND, and it is the actionable half.\nThe arc is FALSE-INCONCLUSIVE work: it makes withheld verdicts resolve. This\nitem points the OPPOSITE way -- it makes host_info_read fire near-universally\nand therefore produces MORE VIOLATED verdicts. A repository that should have\nmoved inconclusive -> clean under an arc fix may instead move\ninconclusive -> VIOLATED, so the arc's payoff would read as smaller than it is,\nand nobody could tell whether a fix underperformed or the clock rows ate the\nimprovement.\n\n  RULE: land this BEFORE the arc's baseline measurements are taken, or AFTER\n  they are complete. NEVER between. Same discipline as A/B-ing the tool rather\n  than the history.\n\nDOWNSTREAM EFFECT ON WI-fosir, recorded there as well. This item ADDS ROWS, so\nmore calls classify, so the uncatalogued-module list gets SHORTER. WI-fosir is\nthe 5-name cap on that same list, so measuring WI-fosir before this lands would\ncalibrate a truncation against a denominator about to shrink -- the identical\ninteraction INV-pusin already had with it."  # 4132
- op: discuss  # 23f0
  at: '2026-08-27T17:10:42Z'  # 23f0
  by: agent  # 23f0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 23f0
  clock: 3  # 23f0
  nonce: 23f0  # 23f0
  message: "KEYING CONSTRAINT FOR THE 15 CLOCK ROWS, measured 2026-08-27 while checking\nwhether the call_construct concept audit bears on this item. It does, and the\nconsequence is a choice of YAML SECTION, not extra work.\n\nRULE: key clock rows under `functions:`. Never `methods:`, never `attributes:`.\n\nWHY, MEASURED WITH THE PRODUCTION PREDICATE (method_starved_modules), on a\nreal erlang call-edge shape into a module this item would create:\n\n    row kind=function                      -> []\n    row kind=method                        -> ['erlang_clock']   FALSE STARVATION\n    row kind=method + function-kind sibling -> ['erlang_clock']   STILL\n\nThe third line is the trap. Route 2 (verify_claims.py:3081) satisfies on the\nCALLED NAME matching a function-kind primitive, so a function-kind row of a\nDIFFERENT name does not rescue a method-kind row of the name actually called.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. method_starved_modules admits a language only if its catalogue\ndeclares at least one method-keyed module. erlang and haskell currently declare\nZERO, so they never reach the construct test. Adding a method-kind clock row\ngives them their first one -- and every erlang call edge stamps `remote`/`local`\nand every haskell edge stamps `application`, none of which is a catalogue kind,\nso route 1 can never be satisfied for those languages. The rows would create\nthe starvation report they are meant to help retire. Measured over 42 surveys:\nerlang 20,673 `remote` + 14,504 `local`, haskell 21,074 `application`, and\n0% of them can ever match a catalogue kind (INV-pimir).\n\n`attributes:` is worse than `methods:`: `attribute` is a third IoPrimitive.kind\nvalue (io_boundary.py:1287, and the field comment at :586 omits it -- WI-fazad),\nand no call_construct value is ever `attribute`, so an attribute-kind row is\nunmatchable by route 1 in EVERY language, not just these two.\n\nALSO RELEVANT, PRE-EXISTING: across 11,691 rust call edges the catalogue matches\nZERO method-kind rows while FUNCTION-kind fires (INV-linub L3/L4). So a\nmethod-kind clock row is inert in rust too, for a different reason.\n\nWHERE THIS IS SAFE ANYWAY. Injecting a method-kind clock row into erlang's\nEXISTING `erlang` module produced no starvation report on the real corpus (186\ncall edges land there), because that module already carries function-kind rows\nwhose names get called. The hazard is specific to a NEW module carrying only\nthe clock row -- which is exactly the shape this item creates for languages\nthat have no clock module catalogued yet.\n\nNOT A BLOCKER, AND DOES NOT REORDER THE ARC. WI-kohig would remove the hazard\nstructurally by folding erlang's `remote`/`local` and haskell's `application`\nonto the construct axis; until then, keying under `functions:` routes around it\nat zero cost."  # 23f0
- op: discuss  # fa75
  at: '2026-08-27T17:21:35Z'  # fa75
  by: agent  # fa75
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fa75
  clock: 4  # fa75
  nonce: fa75  # fa75
  message: "CORRECTION TO THE KEYING CONSTRAINT I POSTED ABOVE. I wrote \"never\n`attributes:`\". That is TOO STRONG and I withdraw that half. The `methods:`\nhalf stands.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. I derived \"never attributes\" from route 1 of\nmethod_starved_modules, which reads `calls` edges only, and from the fact that\n`kind=\"attribute\"` is constructed at io_boundary.py:1287 and never branched on\nanywhere. I then measured 324 `module_attr_ref` edges into javascript's\n`process`/`os` in the corpus surveys with ZERO carrying `meta[\"io_boundary\"]`,\nand read that as \"attribute rows are inert\".\n\nTHE BEHAVIOURAL TEST REFUTES IT. A 6-line javascript fixture reading\n`process.platform`, `process.env.SECRET` and `os.hostname()`:\n\n    $ hypergumbo io-boundaries <fixture>\n    env_read: 1 call(s)\n        process.env (1)\n    host_info_read: 2 call(s)\n        os.hostname (1)\n        process.platform (1)\n\nBoth ATTRIBUTE rows attribute correctly. The survey count was measuring the\nwrong thing: the boundary map is built on a different path from the edge-level\n`meta[\"io_boundary\"]` stamp, so counting that key UNDERSTATES attribution and\nmust not be used as an attribution census. Recording that here because I\nnearly filed a defect off it.\n\nTHE CORRECTED RULE, three parts:\n\n  functions:   genuine free / associated functions. The default.\n  methods:     genuine methods -- EXCEPT in the six languages whose catalogue\n               declares ZERO method-keyed modules today (bash, c, cpp, elixir,\n               erlang, haskell). There a method row creates the language's\n               first method-keyed module, admits it to the starvation check,\n               and its constructs can never satisfy route 1. Conveniently the\n               clock API in all six is genuinely a function or a shell\n               variable, so TRUTH AND SAFETY COINCIDE -- there is no case\n               where this rule forces a false description of the surface.\n  attributes:  genuine attribute reads. Verified live above. bash's existing\n               `shell.hostinfo[attribute]` is the precedent.\n\nSTILL TRUE AND UNCHANGED: rust method-kind rows are inert (INV-linub L3/L4:\n0 of 11,691 call edges match method-kind), so `Instant::elapsed` and\n`SystemTime::elapsed` are catalogued as methods because that is what they ARE,\nwith the inertness owned by INV-linub rather than papered over by mis-keying\nthem as functions."  # fa75
