- op: discuss  # 8b26
  at: '2026-08-27T19:17:27Z'  # 8b26
  by: agent  # 8b26
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8b26
  clock: 6  # 8b26
  nonce: 8b26  # 8b26
  message: "DONE — PR #579, merged to dev at c6def4667c. Re-verified ON THE MERGED TREE,\nnot the branch: 263 host_info_read rows across the fifteen catalogues (156\nbefore), 57 vocabulary tests pass.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. Clock rows in every catalogued language, enumerated per language\nagainst named evidence rather than this item's from-memory list — which the\nitem itself said to do first. Local toolchains where they exist (rustc 1.94.0\nlibrary/std/src/time.rs read directly, Python 3.12.3 introspection, node\nv20.19.6, glibc headers, GNU bash 5.2.21) and allowlisted upstream source\notherwise (golang/go, openjdk/jdk, erlang/otp, elixir-lang/elixir,\nhaskell/time, swift-corelibs-foundation).\n\nMEASURED BEFORE LANDING, as this item required. 28 repos, 2 per language\nacross all 14 changed catalogues, both arms strictly sequential, fresh\nXDG_CACHE_HOME per repo per arm, arm-take proven in both directions\n(156 -> 251 rows at run time):\n\n    host_info_read chains   462 -> 901   (+439, +95%)\n    NO-HOSTINFO verdicts    violated 16 -> 19, inconclusive 9 -> 6\n    CONTROL NO-NET / NO-FSWRITE / NO-SUBPROC   0 moved — PASSES\n\nThe control is the load-bearing half: this change only ADDS host_info_read\nrows, so movement in the other three claims would have been a defect rather\nthan a result. Direction is as this item predicted — recall-INCREASING, more\nclaims fail, and that is the honest answer rather than a regression.\n\nTHREE THINGS THE ENUMERATION FOUND THAT READING THE ROWS WOULD NOT HAVE.\n\n  1. A GAP IN A SHIPPED CATALOGUE. erlang carried os:system_time and\n     os:perf_counter but NOT os:timestamp/0. Catalogue presence is not\n     catalogue coverage — WI-bupor's lesson, recurring.\n  2. CATALOGUE INHERITANCE. _CATALOG_PARENTS gives cpp->c, kotlin->java,\n     scala->java, elixir->erlang. kotlin and scala needed ZERO rows of their\n     own. The first pass was writing duplicates into both and was caught only\n     because cpp had no host_info_read section at all.\n  3. AN ENUMERATION GAP IN MY OWN FIRST CUT, caught by the A/B rather than by\n     review: java.time.LocalDate.now is the corpus's most-called clock (10\n     edges, ahead of Instant.now at 5) and was omitted along with\n     ZonedDateTime / LocalTime / OffsetDateTime. Added, then confirmed on\n     jenkins — the one repo holding those edges, which the 2-per-language\n     cohort had not sampled — at NO-HOSTINFO evidence 15 -> 34 (+127%) with\n     controls unchanged in both arms.\n\nFIVE LANGUAGES MOVED ZERO AND THE CAUSES DIFFER. Reported separately rather\nthan as one comfortable number. rust and scala: correct rows going UNMATCHED\nbecause the analyzer emits module slot `external` for most clock calls (rust\n14 `external` vs 1 `std::time::SystemTime`; scala 14 `external` for\ncurrentTimeMillis) — the resolution gap the arc attacks, not something these\nrows fix. java: the enumeration gap above. javascript: UNVERIFIED and labelled\nso in the row's own note — the corpus holds no Date.now edge at all.\n\nKEYING, pinned by a test: functions: by default; methods: only where genuinely\na method AND the language already declares method-keyed modules; attributes:\nfor genuine attribute reads. bash/c/cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell declare zero\nmethod-keyed modules and must keep it that way (INV-pimir). In all six the\nclock API is genuinely a function or a shell variable, so truth and safety\ncoincide.\n\nADR-0016's boundary table defined host_info_read WITHOUT the clock. Amended in\nplace, stating both accepted consequences: the category now fires almost\nuniversally, and it is recall-increasing.\n\nNO EXISTING module_completeness GRANT WAS FALSIFIED — checked, because a grant\nasserts every I/O primitive is catalogued and a clock is now I/O. Neither\npython's `time` nor rust's `std::time` is granted, and rust's\n`std::time::Duration` grant survives because Duration exposes no clock\n(verified against core/src/time.rs).\n\nWINDOW. This item had to land BEFORE the arc's baseline measurements or after\nthey complete, never between. It has landed before them, so that constraint is\nsatisfied and the baselines are now unblocked on this axis.\n\nFull write-up, including the three instrument bugs that each first read as a\nclean result: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/wi_tubij_clock_surface_08272026.md"  # 8b26
